In the  $xy-$ plane, let  $A(c\cos B,c\sin B); B(0,0); C(a,0)$  define  $\Delta ABC$  (with  $0< \angle{B} < \pi/2$ ).  This leads to  $A_{1}(c\cos B, -c\sin B)$ , as well as: $AB: y=(\tan B)x$  (i); $CC_{1}: y =(-\cot B)x+a\cot B$  (ii);

and  $M = AB \cap CC_{1} = (a\cos^{2} B, a\cos B \sin B)$ .  Computing the coordinates of  $C_{1}(x,y)$  gives us: $a\cos^{2} B = \frac{x+a}{2} \Rightarrow x= a\cos 2B$  (iii); $a\cos B \sin B = \frac{y+0}{2} \Rightarrow y = a\sin 2B$  (iv);

or  $C_{1}(a\cos 2B, a\sin 2B)$ .  If  $A_{1},B,C_{1}$  are collinear and  $|BC_{1}|=2|BA_{1}|$ , then: $\sqrt{(a\cos 2B)^2+(a\sin 2B)^2} = 2\sqrt{(c\cos B)^2 + (-c\sin B)^2} \Rightarrow a=2c$  (v);

and  $A_{1}C_{1}: y+c\sin B = \left(\frac{a\sin 2B+c\sin B}{a\cos 2B - c\cos B}\right)(x-c\cos B)$  (vi).  If  $B(0,0) \in A_{1}C_{1}$ , then (vi) simplifies to: $\tan B = \frac{c\sin B + a\sin 2B}{c\cos B - a\cos 2B} \Rightarrow \tan B = \frac{\sin B+2\sin 2B}{\cos B - 2\cos 2B}$ ;

or  $\frac{\sin B}{\cos B} = \frac{\sin B(1+4\cos B)}{\cos B -2(2\cos^{2} B-1)}$ ;

or  $4\cos^{2}+\cos B = -4\cos^{2} B+\cos B + 2 \Rightarrow \cos^{2} B = \frac{1}{4} \Rightarrow \cos B = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow B = \frac{\pi}{3}$  (vii).

Finally, we check the product of the slopes: $m_{A_{1}B} \cdot m_{A_{1}C} = \frac{0+c\sin B}{0-c\cos B} \cdot \frac{0+c\sin B}{a-c\cos B} = -\frac{sin B}{\cos B} \cdot \frac{\sin B}{2-\cos B} = \frac{\sin^{2} B}{\cos^{2} B-2\cos B} = \frac{3/4}{1/4 - 2(1/2)} = -1$ ;

of which we conclude  $A_{1}B \perp A_{1}C \Rightarrow \angle{CA_{1}B} = \frac{\pi}{2}$ .

CASE I ( $a=b$ ):

We obtain: $a^4(a^4+4) = 2(2a^6) \Rightarrow a^8 - 4a^6 + 4a^4 = a^{4}(a^2-2)^2=0 \Rightarrow \textcolor{red}{a=0, \pm \sqrt{2}}$ ;

of which the non-zero roots  $\notin \mathbb{Q}$ . (QED)


CASE II ( $a \neq b$ ):

Proof by contradiction:

Let  $a,b \in \mathbb{N}$  be distinct positive integers.  We can rewrite the above Diophantine equation according to: $a^{2}b^{2}(a^{2}b^2 +4) + 4= 2(a^6+b^6) + 4 \Rightarrow (a^{2}b^2+2)^2 = 2(a^6+b^6 + 2)$  (i);

which upon inspection of (i) we arrive at: $a^{2}b^{2}+2 = 2^{n} \Rightarrow a^{2}b^{2} = 2(2^{n-1}-1)$  (ii); $a^6 + b^6 + 2 = 2^{2n-1} \Rightarrow a^6 + b^6 = 2(2^{n-1}-1)(2^{n-1}+1)$  (iii);

and substitution of (ii) into (iii) yields  $a^6 + b^6= a^{2}b^{2}(2^{n-1}+1)$  (iv) for  $n \in \mathbb{N}$ .  Further algebraic manipulation produces: $\frac{a^4}{b^2} + 2ab +\frac{b^4}{a^2} = 2^{n-1} + 2ab + 1 \Rightarrow \left(\frac{a^2}{b}+\frac{b^2}{a}\right)^2 = 2^{n-1}+2ab+1 \Rightarrow \frac{a^2}{b}+\frac{b^2}{a} = \sqrt{2^{n-1}+2ab+1}$ ;

or  $\frac{a^2}{b}+\frac{b^2}{a} = \sqrt{(2^{\frac{n-1}{2}})^2 + 2(1)(2^{\frac{n-1}{2}})+1} = \sqrt{(2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}+1)^2} = 2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}+1 \Rightarrow ab = 2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}$  (v).

We require  $n>1$  to be an odd positive integer, as well as  $a=2^{p}, b=2^{q}$  (for  $p,q \in \mathbb{N}$  satisfying  $q/2 < p < 2q$ ).  However, we obtain: $2^{2p-q}+2^{2q-p} = 2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}+1 \Rightarrow$  even integer = odd integer;

which is a contradiction.  Hence, at least one of  $a,b$  must be irrational. (QED)

