Let us rewrite the above inequality according to: $f(x,y) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x^2}} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+y^2}} - \frac{2}{\sqrt{1+xy}}$ such that we wish to prove that  $f_{MAX} \le 0$  for all real  $x,y \in [0,1]$ .  Taking the gradient  $\nabla f = 0$  yields: $f_{x} = -\frac{x}{(1+x^2)^{3/2}}+\frac{y}{(1+xy)^{3/2}}=0$  (i); $f_{y} = -\frac{y}{(1+y^2)^{3/2}}+\frac{x}{(1+xy)^{3/2}}=0$  (ii);

of which equating (i) with (ii) gives  $\frac{x}{y(1+x^2)^{3/2}} = \frac{y}{x(1+y^2)^{3/2}} \Rightarrow x=y=t$ .  A check of the Hessian Matrix of  $f$  at the critical point  $(x,y)=(t,t)$  yields: $F(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} f_{xx} && f_{xy}  f_{yx} && f_{yy} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{3x^2}{(1 + x^2)^{5/2}} - \frac{1}{(1 + x^2)^{3/2}} - \frac{3y^2}{2(1 + xy)^{5/2}} && \frac{2-xy}{2(1+xy)^{5/2}}  \frac{2-xy}{2(1+xy)^{5/2}} && \frac{3y^2}{(1 + y^2)^{5/2}} - \frac{1}{(1 + y^2)^{3/2}} - \frac{3x^2}{2(1 + xy)^{5/2}} \end{bmatrix}$ which has distinct eigenvalues  $\lambda = 0, \frac{t^2-2}{(t^2+1)^{5/2}}$  which are both  $\le 0 \Rightarrow$  negative semi-definite (or a local maximum at  $(t,t)$  for  $t \in [0,1]$ ).  This gives us  $f_{MAX} = f(t,t) = 0$ , which is the result we wished to prove.

QED

