First, I will prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** We have the following two results:


- $a_1+\ldots+a_{j-1} \ge a_j-1$  for all  $2 \le j \le n,$  and
- $a_1+\ldots+a_{j-1} \ge a_j$  for all  $2 \le j \le n$  such that  $a_{j+1}+\ldots+a_n$  is even.
[/list]
*Proof.* First, observe that  $a_i \ge \tfrac{a_j}{2^{j-i}}$  for all  $1 \le i<j \le n$  by repeatedly applying the fact that  $a_{i+1} \le 2a_i$  for all  $1 \le i \le n-1.$  Therefore, it follows that
\begin{align*}
    a_1+\ldots+a_{j-1} &\ge \frac{a_j}{2^{j-1}}+\frac{a_j}{2^{j-2}}+\ldots+\frac{a_j}{2} 
    &= a_j\left(\frac{1}{2}+\ldots+\frac{1}{2^{j-1}}\right) 
    &= a_j\left(1-\frac{1}{2^{j-1}}\right) 
    &= a_j-\frac{a_j}{2^{j-1}}
\end{align*}
Therefore, it suffices to show that  $a_j \le 2^{j-1}$  and strict inequality holds if  $a_{j+1}+\ldots+a_n$  is even. Indeed, we have
\begin{align*}
    a_j &\le 2a_{j-1} 
    &\le 4a_{j-2} 
    &\qquad\vdots 
    &\le 2^{j-1}a_1 
    &= 2^{j-1}.
\end{align*}
In order for equality to hold, we need to have  $a_k=2^{k-1}$  for all  $1 \le k \le j$  from which it follows that
\begin{align*}
    a_1+a_2+\ldots+a_j &= 1+2+\ldots+2^{j-1} 
    &= 2^j-1,
\end{align*}
which is odd. Therefore, if  $a_{j+1}+\ldots+a_n$  is even, this is a contradiction (because then  $a_1+\ldots+a_n$  would be odd), implying that  $a_j<2^{j-1}$  if  $a_{j+1}+\ldots+a_n$  is even. 

Hence, I have proven the claimed results.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, let us perform the following algorithm:
[list=1]
- Place  $a_n$  in some pile.
- Place  $a_{n-1}, \ldots, a_1$  in that order such that we always place a number in the smaller pile thus far (with ties broken arbitrarily).

By the lemma, if at some point the piles *do not* have the same total sum, we are guaranteed to make the smaller pile have a sum greater than or equal to the larger pile in some number of more steps (possibly all of the rest of them) because if the last number placed in the larger pile was  $a_j,$  then the difference between the piles at that point could have been at most  $a_{j}-1.$  

If at some point, the piles *do* have the same total sum, then after we add the next number to one of the piles, we are also guaranteed to make the smaller pile have a sum greater than or equal to the larger pile in some number of steps because if the next number placed is  $a_j,$  the difference between the two piles at that point has to be  $a_j.$  

Hence, when we place the  $a_1$  in a pile, it will bring the sum of that pile greater than or equal to the sum of the other pile. If the difference between the two piles at that point is  $2$  or more, then  $a_1$  should have been placed in the other pile, and if it is  $1,$  then the total sum of  $a_1, \ldots, a_n$  will be odd, contradiction, so the total sums of the two piles at that point must be equal, as desired.  $\square$

